                                                                          1/7
"E" commands: ED x,y,z - edit at position x, staff y, code number z.
              EX - exit from score program.
              EDI - EDit Input mode.

 ******  various ways to get into edit mode  ********

ED x,y,c1,c2,c3 - edit at position x, staff y, up to 3 code numbers.
ST x,c1,c2,c3   - edit on staff x, up to 3 code number. (See S.HLP also.)
I x             - edit beginning with item number x.
N x             - as above but with no typeout of parameter numbers.

 ***** ED(it) x,y,c1,c2,c3
A vertical line of dots will appear at the position given and all items
within 5 spaces of the line on that staff will come up for editing.
To pass over an item type <enter>.  See below for method of changing the
various parameters. (Typing "ED" alone assumes x,y values of last item edited.)

If the staff number (y) = 0, or > 32, items on all staves may be edited.
If 1000 is added to y, editing will begin on staff y and continue on up.

If code numbers are given, the item nearest the line with that number
will come up.  Following that, all other items with that code number near 
the vertical line will come up.
*
                                                                          2/7
        ***** S(et) and A(lign) ---------

  If you type 'S' while in ANY form of edit mode, you will go into 'ED' mode,
  and the vertical line will jump to the position of the current item under
  consideration.  Type <enter> to proceed to other items.  At this point
  typing 'A' will automatically align the current item to the position of
  the item at 'SET'.  Type 'X' to exit from any edit mode.


 *****  'I' x = edit item number x.
                This may be typed at any time while in edit mode.
                The item indicated will next come up unless the item
                currently being edited has been changed.  In this
                case 'X', 'C' or <enter> must be given before the next
                'I' is typed.  This will hold true for all the
                various ways of getting into edit mode.


 *****'N' x (N=noprint) is same as 'I', but parameters and prompts will not
                appear.  Type 'T' to return to normal type-out.



*
 ***** 'ST' n c1 c2 c3 =edit staff n, find up to 3 code nums.             3/7
           If n > 32, then all staves are searched for code numbers.
           If no code numbers are given, all items on staff may be edited.

           Add 1000 to 'n' to edit all staves beginning with staff n. 

    ********************  How to change parameters  *********************
 Changing position parameters only (P3, P4, etc.):

  L,R,U,D followed by n, moves n spaces to left, right; or n steps up, down.
    In many instances, 2 directions may be given at once.
    LL,RR,UU,DD -- moves 1/2 space left, right or 1/2 step up, down.
    LI,RI,UI,DI n -- moves n inches left, right, up, down.
    LC,RC,UC,DC n -- moves n centimeters left, right, up, down.

  After an 'L' or 'R' move has been made, the letter alone (no number)
  will suffice to move the same value again.  The same applies to
  the 'U-D' pair.

  The cursor keys can also be used to move items in a similar fashion.

  The F4 key can be used to lock onto left end of certain items while the
  F5 key can be used to lock onto the right end -- for adjusting the length
  or tilt of the items with the various LRUD and cursor key commands.
*
                                                                          4/7
 Changing all parameters:

  Pn1,n1  Pn2,n2  Pn3,n3  etc. (type pairs of numbers to change parameters)
                The first of each pair must be the parameter number
                and the second will be the new value for that param.

         If 2 zeros are added after a parameter number (i.e. 600 instead 
         of 6), the second number of the pair will be added to the current
         value of that parameter instead of merely replacing.  This is useful
         for extending P6 of slurs or beams, etc.

         If 3 zeros are added to a parameter number, that parameter will
         be made equal to the parameter of following number.
         Thus '5000 4' makes P5=P4.

         <enter> ends edit and moves to next item for edit
         'X' ends edit, exits to standard mode. 'Xn' moves to next item.
         'C' saves old and new forms, i.e. "copies" item.
         'CC' copies and stays in edit mode.
         'C' n makes copy and moves on to item n.
         'CX' makes copy and moves on to next item in sequence.
         'DEL' (or <delete>) deletes item and moves on to next item.

*
                                                                          5/7
 ***  : saving a string of commands with the use of an apostrophe or colon ***

It is possible to save up 3 different strings (to 23 chars.) of commands on
a single line of input by preceding each string with an apostrophe or colon.
This feature is usually used while in edit mode, but it could be used
for entering new items.
        Example:   i 54                      (we begin to edit item 54)
                   '5 2  7 1 ''l7 u2  '''cn  (the commands are entered, and
                                              the first string is executed.)
                (note that no space may follow an apostrophe)

      From this point on typing ; will always execute "5 2  7 1."
      To execute the other commands, type ;; for the 2nd and ;;; for the 3rd.

  These 3 commands may now be used separately at any appropriate time by
  typing ;, ;;, or ;;;.  ;;;; will display the saved command strings.

  Any time the apostrophes are typed again, the original strings are replaced.
   (This system works for any one-line command.  It will not work with the
    group edit commands, which require more than one line of input.)
 ****** The apostrophe (') and colon (:) are interchangeable.


*
                                                                          6/7

 ***** EDI - ED(it)I(nput mode) *****

   After typing EDI you will be prompted for a staff number and the
   horizontal area to be edited.  If only the staff number is given, the
   entire line will be edited.

   At this point the material on the staff will be treated as if it had
   just been created by the Input mode and you had asked to backup to
   correct some lines.  Typing the letter 'B' will backup to the beginning
   of the input list.  The letter 'G' (=go) will jump to the end of the
   input procedure.

   REMEMBER that in most cases the LJ (lineup and justify) command must be
   used after using EDI, so that the notes on other staves will be properly
   aligned.

   EDI will not work on a staff where there are simultaneous rhythms of
   different value (counterpoint).  In such a case it may be possible to
   temporarily move the pertinent notes, etc. to some unused staff before
   using EDI.


*
                                                                          7/7

ENH  = group edit command to alter notes enharmonically.

 ***** While editing a note, ENH will alter that note enharmonically.
          ENH1  moves note to step above.
          ENH-1 moves note to step below.



Extended letter commands:

     These commands are shortcuts for creating various items.  They should
     normally be followed by 2 or 3 numbers (parameters 2, 3, 4).

         esp 3 45 14   will put "espr." on staff 3 at position 45,
                       vertical position 14.

END1 = 1st ending
END2 = 2nd ending
ESP  = "espr."


